td-txt conformance corpus
=========================

The cases the td-txt conformance gate runs (see ../tests/conformance.rs). Three
provenances live here, and the harness (../src/lib.rs) reads all three into one
case type — argv, input files, stdin, expected stdout/stderr/status.

Why not one upstream corpus, the way td-sh vendors Oils' spec files: grep and
sed have no single self-contained suite with recorded goldens. What they have is
better in one way and worse in another — their upstream tests are DATA files
whose expected result is already written down (so no oracle binary is needed in
an offline sandbox), but that data covers the REGEX ENGINE and whole SCRIPTS,
not the programs' option surface. Hence the split below.


Vendored from GNU grep, UNMODIFIED
----------------------------------
  gnu-grep/bre.tests
  gnu-grep/ere.tests
  gnu-grep/spencer1.tests
  gnu-grep/khadafy.regexp
  gnu-grep/khadafy.lines

  Upstream:  https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/grep/grep-3.11.tar.xz
  SHA-256:   1db2aedde89d0dea42b16d9528f894c8d15dae4e190b59aecc78f5a951276eab
  Path:      tests/<file>  (copied byte-for-byte)
  License:   GPL-3.0-or-later (GNU grep). Copyright the Free Software Foundation.

  Selection rule (reproducible from the pin): the DATA files of grep's own
  regression suite — the three Spencer-format `.tests` files plus the Khadafy
  pair. Everything else under tests/ is a shell script driven by gnulib's
  init.sh, which needs a shell, a built grep tree and `$srcdir`; those are the
  scripts this corpus deliberately does not import.

  Row grammar (`<status>@<pattern>@<input>`) and the invocation come from
  upstream's own awk drivers at that pin. bre.awk emits

      status=$(echo '<input>' | { grep -e '<pattern>' >/dev/null 2>&1; echo $?; })

  and ere.awk/spencer1.awk the same with `-E`. So this corpus runs
  `grep [-E] -e <pattern>` with `<input>` (plus a newline, as `echo` writes it)
  on stdin and asserts ONLY the exit status — upstream discards the output. A
  row with a FOURTH field is upstream's own "expected non-conformance" marker
  (`TO CORRECT`, `NO ALTERNATION`): its awk driver does not assert those rows,
  and neither does the reader here.

  The Khadafy case is upstream's `khadafy` test: one ERE read with `-f` over 32
  spellings, all of which must come back unchanged.


Vendored from GNU sed, UNMODIFIED
---------------------------------
  gnu-sed/<name>.sed  +  <name>.inp  +  <name>.good   (35 triples)

  Upstream:  https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/sed/sed-4.2.2.tar.gz
  SHA-256:   fea0a94d4b605894f3e2d5572e3f96e4413bcad3a085aae7367c2cf07908b2ff
  Path:      testsuite/<file>  (copied byte-for-byte)
  License:   GPL-3.0-or-later (GNU sed). Copyright the Free Software Foundation.

  Selection rule (reproducible from the pin): exactly the test names listed in
  the FIRST, uniform recipe of upstream's testsuite/Makefile.tests —

      enable sep inclib 8bit 8to7 newjis xabcx dollar noeol bkslashes numsub
      head madding mac-mf empty xbxcx xbxcx3 recall recall2 xemacs appquit
      fasts uniq manis linecnt khadafy allsub flipcase space modulo y-bracket
      y-newline insert brackets amp-escape

  — the ones upstream runs as plain

      LC_ALL=C sed -f <name>.sed < <name>.inp   |cmp -   <name>.good

  with no other flag or environment. That is why this pin is 4.2.2 rather than
  the current 4.9: 4.9 converted most of these into shell scripts, and the
  triples are the part that needs no shell. Names upstream runs differently
  (`0range` needs `-s`, `badenc`/`utf8-*` need a UTF-8 locale, `stdin` pipes two
  seds) are deliberately NOT here: importing them would mean encoding an
  invocation this corpus cannot read off the data.

  Why 4.2.2's goldens are still the right target: these cases exercise POSIX sed
  behavior that did not change between 4.2.2 and 4.9. Where td-txt is scored
  against NEWER sed behavior, that lives in sed-cli.test.txt below, whose
  goldens come from 4.9.


td-txt-authored case files
--------------------------
  grep-cli.test.txt   the grep option surface, prefixes, exit statuses, refusals
  sed-cli.test.txt    the sed command language, options, in-place editing, refusals
  divergence.test.txt where td-txt deliberately differs from GNU

  grep-cli.test.txt and sed-cli.test.txt each end with a block derived from td's
  OWN usage: every grep/sed invocation shape the image's generated /etc scripts
  and the recipes' build steps actually make, one case per shape, each paired
  with the negative that shows the shape DISCRIMINATES. That block is what makes
  the corpus a statement about the image rather than about the option list: the
  /bin/grep those scripts call is this binary. Re-derive the shapes by scanning
  the generated scripts (`recipes`' `script_applets_are_covered` derives applet
  usage from the same text) and the recipe step strings.

  grep-cli.test.txt ends with one further block, the `rootcheck td-volume` cases.
  Those score a shape that is not merely USED by the image but was MOVED to it:
  /etc/rootcheck decided whether the persistent volume was mounted read-only with
  a busybox `awk` field test, and that predicate is now an ERE. `ro` has to match
  as a whole comma-delimited option, so the block is mostly negatives —
  `errors=remount-ro`, `rootcontext=`, `rofoo` and a `subvol=/@ro` value all carry
  the letters without being the option. `recipes` pins the same pattern text in
  `rootcheck_pins_the_td_volume_ere`, which is the other half of the binding: that
  end proves the script still spells what this end scores.

  Three further blocks come from DIFFERENTIAL FUZZING against grep 3.11 rather than
  from reading the option list — `grep -w` over a pattern that can match a shorter
  span, BRE anchor-vs-literal (`^^`), and POSIX leftmost-longest across a bounded
  repeat. All three were wrong ANSWERS, not refusals, and none of the shapes is
  exotic (`-w` with a `*`/`?` pattern; a doubled `^`; a `\{n,m\}` after a `*`). The
  hand-picked cases had missed all three — which is the point. How to run another
  campaign:

    - generate random patterns from a BRE/ERE grammar and random short inputs,
      run `grep [-E] <flags> -e PAT file` under both binaries with LC_ALL=C, and
      compare status + stdout + stderr;
    - BUCKET the divergences before reading them. Raw counts are dominated by
      noise: a deliberate refusal (`-b`) and GNU's own `warning:` notices. The
      quantifier-on-a-zero-width-assertion corner (`\<*`, `\b?`) used to belong on
      that list -- excluding it took one run from ~450 divergences to 3 -- and no
      longer does: it is modelled now, in all four dialects, and what remains is
      recorded below. Bucketing it away is how one campaign hands the next a
      corner nobody has read; the model that replaced it was got WRONG twice
      first, both times by probing it through a single syntactic frame;
    - the bucket that matters is "both accepted the pattern and the ANSWERS
      differ". Everything else is a refusal-text question.

  The fuzzer is deliberately NOT committed: it needs a GNU oracle binary, which
  is not a declared input of any recipe, so it could not run in the offline gate.
  Its findings are committed instead — as cases with goldens captured from the
  oracle, which is what the gate can actually check.

  What the fuzzer did NOT reach is worth knowing too. Its grammar never emits an
  EMPTY pattern, and an empty pattern short-circuited the regex engine entirely,
  so the whole `match_all` path was outside the search space — and that path had
  the same `-w` bug in its own form. A reviewer found it by reading. A generator
  only covers the shapes its grammar can produce, so the block below it
  (`-w` over an empty pattern) exists because someone looked at the code instead.

  Its inputs were also all SHORT, which hid a whole class: what a change costs on
  a long line. Making the matcher explore every end for leftmost-longest turned
  `grep '.*=.*=.*'` on a 641-byte line into `too complex` (exit 2), and 798 green
  cases said nothing, because not one of them had a line that long. Fuzz LENGTH,
  not just shape, and diff against the PREVIOUS td-txt as well as against GNU: the
  question "did something that used to answer stop answering" is invisible when
  the oracle is the only comparison. The long-line block below is that regression
  test.

  The same campaign against SED, whose corpus had had none, found six — and its
  noise bucket was one flag rather than a regex corner. `--posix` dominated every
  early run, because GNU's `--posix` disables GNU's own extensions and refuses
  twelve constructs td-txt accepts (`first~step`, `addr,+N`, `addr,~N`, `0,/re/`,
  `s///I`, `s///M`, `Q`, `T`, `z`, `F`, one-liner `a text`, `q5`) while turning
  `\U\L\u\l\E` and BRE `\+ \? \| \w` into literals. td-txt implements `--posix`
  for exactly ONE of those (`N` at end of input) and silently ignores the rest, so
  every script that mixed the flag with an extension read as a divergence.
  Excluding the flag took 750 cases from 20 divergences to 1. Make it the first
  thing you bucket, and note that the gap it hides is itself a finding: an option
  accepted and mostly ignored is the "silent no-op" ../src/sed.rs's header says
  this program never has. Fixing that is its own landing.

  Sed rewards fuzzing the SEPARATOR, not just the script. Four of the six bugs
  needed `-z` or an input with no final newline to show at all: `l` wrapped with a
  newline where GNU wraps with the record separator, `M` anchored at a newline
  under `-z` where GNU anchors at the NUL, and `q` paid a separator debt that
  reaching end of input must leave unpaid. Generate unterminated inputs and
  NUL-separated ones as a matter of course; a corpus of newline-terminated lines
  cannot see any of it.

  Two refinements of that, each of which cost a round of review to learn. First,
  fuzz the commands that put a separator INSIDE the pattern space. `M` is POSIX
  REG_NEWLINE relative to the record separator, and the first pass concluded that
  its anchor half must go inert under `-z` because no record contains its own
  separator -- true of a record, false of a pattern space, because `N` joins two
  records WITH one. Every `-z`+`M` case in the first pass omitted `N`, so 232 green
  cases agreed with a wrong rule. Second, fuzz more than one output stream and more
  than one input file, because sed's state is a mix of per-run and per-file and a
  single-file, single-stream generator cannot tell which is which.

  What `M` actually is, since two rounds of this branch got it wrong: GNU
  implements it as TWO mechanisms, and a pattern can tell them apart.

  - libc's REG_NEWLINE, bound to `\n` whatever the separator is. `^`/`$` gain the
    embedded newlines and `.` / a non-matching bracket list lose them -- but
    NOTHING else does, so `\W` and an explicit `[\n]` still match one.
  - Confinement to the segments between separators, which GNU needs only when the
    separator is not a newline, libc having no way to express it. There the anchors
    move to that byte and nothing consumes it: not `.`, not `\W`, not `\s`, not a
    named `\x00`, and no backreference spans one.

  Both of those are properties of the CALL, not of the pattern -- which is the third
  thing this branch got wrong about `M`, and the missing fuzz axis was the COMMAND
  FORM. GNU confines a SUBSTITUTION to the segment it is working in; an ADDRESS
  matches the whole pattern space. So `sed -z -n -e N -e '/a.b/Mp'` matches across
  the NUL while `s/a.b/X/M` on the same space does not, and the same split holds for
  `\W`, a named `\x00` and `[^x]`. The BUFFER ANCHORS split the same way, and not as
  you would guess from any other regex flavour: to an address ``\` ``/`\'` are
  indistinguishable from `^`/`$` and move to the record separator with them, while a
  substitution moves them only for a SEGMENT. So with a newline separator `/\`b/M`
  matches the pattern space `a\nb` and `s/\`b/X/M` finds nothing in it, and
  `s/\`a/X/Mg` over `a\na` rewrites ONE `a` where `s/^a/X/Mg` rewrites both. Under
  `-z` the segment moves them for both callers. The REG_NEWLINE rules above (`.` and
  a non-matching list losing `\n`) apply to both callers either way.

  One more thing splits the two paths, and no rule about `M` predicts it: a
  BACKREFERENCE anywhere in the pattern confines an ADDRESS as well. `sed -z -n -e
  'N;N' -e '/\(.\)\1/Mp'` over `a\0\0b\0` finds nothing, while `/../Mp` and
  `/\(a\)\x00/Mp` over the same pattern space both match -- and `/\(a\)\x00\1*/Mp`
  does not, though `\1*` need consume nothing, so it is the PRESENCE of a backref and
  not its own match that does it. That is glibc's backref matcher, not something sed
  documents; td-txt reproduces it with one compile-time flag rather than recording a
  divergence, because the corpus's oracle is what GNU 4.9 does here. A match wholly
  inside one segment is unaffected, and with a newline separator (no segment) a
  doubled NUL is an ordinary pair to a backref.

  So under `-z` a newline inside a record stays ordinary to `\W` while the NUL is
  invisible to everything a substitution can consume. Probing only `.` and `[^...]`
  cannot distinguish the two mechanisms, because those are the one place both apply
  -- probe `\W` against BOTH separators, which is the single cheapest
  discriminator. And confinement has to hold for the greedy-repeat fast path too,
  which consumes bytes without taking the ordinary single-byte step: `s/a.b/X/M`
  refused a NUL that `s/.*/X/M` swallowed.

  One ordering rule that only `r`/`R` against the quit commands can expose: `N` at
  END of input prints the pattern space BEFORE flushing the append queue, the
  reverse of every other path, `n` at end of input included. Two commands that both
  write, in the same cycle, is the shape to generate -- a queue and a print each
  look right alone.

  The inventory of per-run versus per-file state, which the `-s` bugs came out of:

  - Per RUN, surviving every operand: the output streams and their owed
    separators. The debt is per output TARGET (GNU keeps one per filename), so `w
    /dev/stdout` is a different stream from auto-print even though both reach the
    same fd, a `w` file has its own, and `/dev/stderr` is an ordinary target with
    raw bytes and no forced newline. `q` settles the auto-print stream alone.
  - Per FILE under `-s`/`-i`: line numbers, range state, the hold space and every
    REWINDABLE `R` read position. GNU clears the hold's TEXT but keeps its
    terminator flag, which shows only after an unterminated file. And it restarts an
    `R` source with `rewind(3)`, which fails silently on a stream that cannot seek:
    a pipe or fifo keeps its place across the boundary while a regular file starts
    over. GNU also aliases the literal `/dev/stdin` to its own stdin stream, which
    is not among the streams it rewinds however seekable that stream is -- so with
    stdin redirected FROM A REGULAR FILE, `R /dev/stdin` does not rewind while `R
    /proc/self/fd/0` and `R /dev/fd/0`, naming the same file, do. That last pair
    cannot go in the corpus: which of the two answers is right depends on whether
    the harness hands the case a pipe or a file.
  - `R` cursors are keyed by NAME, not per command: two `R f` commands share one
    cursor and advance it twice in a cycle, while an `r f` beside them is unrelated.
  - And `w /dev/stdout` still means the real standard output under `-i`, where the
    auto-print stream is the replacement buffer.

  What ends a command's ARGUMENT is the same question one step in, and the answer is
  not "the end of the command" for any of the three arguments that look like text. A
  LABEL (`:`, `b`, `t`, `T`) ends at a BLANK as much as at `;`, `}`, `#` or a newline,
  so `sed -n ':a p'` is the label `a` and then `p`, and `b a p` jumps to `a`. `v`'s
  optional version ends there too -- `v;p` runs the `p` -- and GNU compares it with
  glibc's `strverscmp` against its OWN version, so `v 4.10` is refused while `v 4.9`
  is not, `v 4a` is refused because `a` sorts above `.` while `v 4-9` is accepted
  because `-` sorts below it, and an empty argument compares as 4.0. td-txt compares
  against the GNU LEVEL it implements rather than its own version number, since
  comparing against 0.1.0 would refuse the `v 4.2` real scripts carry. And `s`'s
  FLAGS end only at a command terminator: a blank separates flags rather than ending
  them (`s/a/b/ g p` is both), an unknown letter is `unknown option to `s'` rather
  than the next command, and `g`, `p` and the occurrence number may not repeat while
  `i`/`I`/`m`/`M` may.

  One acceptance rule the command-form axis turned up on its own: GNU requires a
  command SEPARATOR after every command that does not consume the rest of the line,
  so `sed -n 'gp'` is an error and not `g` then `p`, and so is `g p`. `;`, a newline,
  `}`, `#` and end-of-script separate. A filename, `a`/`i`/`c` text and a comment
  need nothing because they swallow the line; a label and `v`'s version need nothing
  because GNU asks for nothing after them (`:a p` is a label then a command); and
  `s` needs nothing because its own flag loop enforces the same terminator set with
  its own diagnostic, while `y` has no flags and does need a separator. td-txt accepted all twenty of those juxtapositions, which is how a
  generator emitting `/re/Mgp` -- `g` is no address flag -- produced 34 divergences
  in one seed and looked like a regex bug.

  `-` is not stdin everywhere it can appear. It is stdin as an OPERAND and for `-f`
  (where GNU prefers stdin even when a file called `-` exists), and an ordinary
  filename to `r`, `R` and `w` (where it always was) and to an operand under `-i`
  (there being no rewriting a pipe). So `R -` reads a file named `-` and reads
  nothing at all when there is none, silently, because a missing `R` source is not
  an error.

  The escape VOCABULARY is decoded before anything else parses the text, and what
  the decoded byte means then depends on who reads it. GNU knows `\n`, `\t`, `\r`,
  `\f`, `\v`, `\a`, `\cX`, `\dNNN`, `\oNNN` and `\xHH` -- and NOT `\e`, worth
  pinning because it is the one every other tool has. In a PATTERN the byte is
  inserted RAW, arriving as regex SYNTAX rather than as a literal: `\x2e` is the
  metacharacter dot, `\x2a` quantifies, `\x5e` anchors, `\x5b` is an unmatched
  bracket, `\x5c` is a trailing backslash, `\x5cw` is the word class, and
  `[a\x2dz]` is a RANGE. That is also why an escape works inside a bracket
  expression, where the regex parser itself takes a backslash literally: the
  decoder ran first, so the bracket never sees one. A REPLACEMENT decodes to a
  LITERAL instead -- `\x26` is an ampersand that does not stand for the match and
  `\x5c1` is a backslash and a `1`, not a backreference -- while `a`/`i`/`c` text
  and both halves of `y///` decode to plain bytes, `y`'s operand lengths being
  measured AFTER the decoding (`y/X\x59/12/` is two for two). An escape GNU does
  not know keeps its backslash for the next parser in a pattern (`\w`, `\1`) and
  sheds it everywhere else (`a A\qB` appends `AqB`). Three more rules earn their
  place: the one-liner `a text` form eats a LEADING backslash before decoding, so
  `a \t.` appends `t.` while `a \\x41` appends `A`; a decoded byte is never read
  again, so `\x5cx41` is a backslash and `x41`; and `\c` takes exactly one
  character, refusing a nested escape (`\c\q` is `recursive escaping after \c not
  allowed`) except the doubled `\c\\`, which is 0x1c. A `\c` with NOTHING to
  control leaves a BARE BACKSLASH, and one rule covers all three readers because
  each resolves that differently: the regex compiler rejects it (`Trailing
  backslash`), a replacement keeps it, a text buffer drops it -- which is why
  `y/X/\c/` is a length error and `s/X/A\c/` emits `A\`. FILENAMES decode
  nothing: neither implementation reads an escape in an `r`/`w` target, so `r
  \x72f` reads a file literally named `\x72f` in both. An earlier draft of that
  last note claimed otherwise and a reviewer caught it; the probe is one line, so
  run it rather than reasoning by analogy from the bracket case.

  A REPETITION OPERATOR needs something to repeat, and the two tools disagree
  about what happens when it has nothing -- which is the first place their regex
  grammars are not one grammar with two spellings. `Options::strict_repeats` is
  that split (sed true, grep false); it is not a knob but which syntax bits glibc
  was handed. A zero-width assertion is never something to repeat, so an operator
  after one is in the same position as an operator after nothing at all:

    * in a BRE the operator is then a LITERAL character -- `\B\?a` reads its `\?`
      exactly as `\?a` does and selects the text `?a`, and `\B*a` selects `*a`.
      grep says `warning: stray \ before ?` where sed says nothing, and neither
      refuses. A leading `^` behaves the same way (`^*` selects lines starting
      with `*`), which is the older rule this one generalizes;
    * sed reads it that way ANYWHERE, not only at the start of a branch: `x\b*`
      selects the `x*` in `x*y`, `[x]\b*` too, and a second operator quantifies
      the LITERAL, so `q\b**` selects `q*` where `qa**` is refused. grep parts
      company past the branch start and repeats the assertion instead (`x\B\+b`
      selects `xb` and not `x b`);
    * an INTERVAL is the exception: sed refuses `\{` with nothing to repeat
      (`\{2\}a`, `\<\{2\}a`, `x\b\{2\}y`) where grep warns `stray \ before {` and
      reads a literal brace, so `grep '\{2\}'` selects lines containing `{2}`;
    * an ERE keeps its operators operators, so the two dialects part again: sed
      refuses every one of them on an assertion -- `^*`, `^?`, `^{2}`, `$*`,
      `\`*`, `\b+`, `\B*`, `\<*` -- mid-pattern included (`x\b*b`), which a group
      around the assertion lifts (`(^)*a` compiles); grep REPEATS the assertion,
      and asserting a position n times at one position asserts it once, so only
      the lower bound survives: `\b*a` leaves nothing behind, `\b+a` still
      anchors. An operator with nothing at all before it is DROPPED there rather
      than made literal (`grep -E '*a'` is the pattern `a`), except that a branch
      emptied by dropping may not close a group: `(*)` and `(a|*)` are an
      unmatched `(` where `(*|a)` and `()` are fine. A brace grep cannot read as
      an interval is a literal, content and all, so `-E '{}a'` selects `{}a`
      while `-E 'a{}'` -- a brace WITH something to repeat -- is an error.

  Stacking is otherwise unchanged and is the same two rules as before, both of
  them SED's: its BRE refuses a second `*` or interval after ANY operator (`a**`,
  `a\+*`, `a\{2\}*`, `\(a\)**`) while TAKING `\+` and `\?` there (`a*\+`, `a\?\?`),
  and its ERE stacks all four freely (`a**`, `a+*`, `a{1,2}{1,2}`). grep refuses
  none of them in either dialect.

  A BACKREFERENCE names a group that must be in scope AND must have participated.
  Out of scope is a compile error in both tools: a group whose `\)` is unreached
  (`\(\1\)`, ERE `(\1)`), and a group in an EARLIER branch of an alternation the
  reference is not in (`(a)|b\1`, `(a)|(b)\1`) -- the second becomes legal again
  once the alternation closes, so `((a)|b)\2` compiles. Participation is a
  matching rule instead: a group that matched zero times has no text and the
  reference FAILS rather than matching the empty string it has no claim to, so
  `(x)*\1` selects nothing in a line without an `x`, while `(x*)\1` -- a group
  that matched empty on purpose -- matches.

  The gaps this campaign found and did NOT fix, recorded here so the next round
  starts from the probe results rather than re-deriving them. All but the first
  have corpus cases carrying GNU's goldens, listed as xfail; `--posix`'s two cases
  PASS, because what is missing there is a restriction rather than an answer, and
  the probe table below is its specification instead:

  - `--posix`, above: implemented for one behaviour of twelve-plus, silently
    ignored for the rest. `POSIXLY_CORRECT` selects the same mode in GNU and is not
    read at all here (`POSIXLY_CORRECT=1 sed '$!d;N'` over two lines prints nothing
    in GNU and the second line here), and GNU refuses `v` outright under the flag
    (`unknown command: `v'`) while clearing posix mode is `v`'s documented side
    effect when it is allowed -- neither of which td-txt has anywhere to put until
    the mode exists.

  - GNU opens a `w` target while it PARSES the command, not on first write. So the
    file is created (and an existing one truncated) even if no write ever reaches
    it, an unopenable target is exit 4 from a command no cycle runs, and it
    outranks a script error LATER in the script while an EARLIER one outranks it
    (`sed -n -e 'w /nodir/f' -e '{'` is exit 4; `-e ZZ -e 'w /nodir/f'` is exit 1).
    That last ordering is the constraint: it means opening after the parse finishes
    is not a fix, and td-txt's parser cannot currently fail with any status but 1.
    It covers the `s///w` FLAG by the same route, which is the thing a
    parser-shape generator keeps rediscovering: a `w` filename runs to the
    end of the line, so any generator that puts a `/` after one hands GNU a path
    whose directory does not exist and gets exit 4 from a command no cycle runs.

  - GNU opens each operand only when it reaches it, so a `q` early in the script
    means a later unreadable operand is never opened and never reported. td-txt's
    non-`-s` path reads every operand up front to resolve `$`, so it cannot tell a
    failure GNU would have seen from one it would have quit before reaching. Two
    consequences, both pinned as cases: it prints a diagnostic GNU never does, and
    where the failure comes FIRST the quit code wins there while GNU's failure wins.
    The rule, which the `-s`/`-i` path does honour, is that a failure ALREADY SEEN
    outranks the quit code whatever it is -- `sed -s -n Q7 /nosuch A` is 2, not 7 --
    while a quit reached first keeps its own code. Making the eager path lazy means
    teaching `$` to look ahead one operand.

    A third consequence, and the cheapest probe for the whole gap: `F` names the
    wrong file as soon as the script contains a `$`. GNU's lookahead has ALREADY
    opened the next operand and `F` reports whatever is open, so `sed -n -e '$d' -e
    F in0 in1 in2` prints `in1 in2` in GNU and `in0 in1` here; without the `$` both
    print all three. It is one xfail case, and it is the shape to check first if
    anyone makes the eager read lazy, because getting `F` right is the same question
    as getting the lookahead right.

  - A `\c` with nothing to control eats an `a` text's own TERMINATOR: GNU
    accumulates the text WITH its trailing newline and decodes that whole buffer,
    so `a\<newline>\c` appends `J` -- the newline upper-cased and XORed with 0x40
    -- and no newline after it. td-txt's buffer ends where the newline begins, the
    separator being appended by the writer, so there is nothing there to consume.
    One xfail case, `a` only: `i` and `c` substitute the separator for the text's
    last byte and so agree whatever it ends with. Matching it means the text
    carrying its own terminator, which puts back in play the `-z` asymmetry td-txt
    matches today (`i foo` ends in a NUL where `a foo` ends in a newline). The
    neighbouring shapes are NOT gaps: a text ending in an unpaired backslash is
    emitted raw, and a continuation collapses only across an `-e` boundary.

  - What an escaped DELIMITER means, in three shapes that predate this campaign
    and share one cause: GNU's `match_slash` collapses `\<delim>` for EVERY
    delimiter, having special-cased a regex `\n` first, while td-txt decides from
    the delimiter's character class. So an alphanumeric delimiter does not
    collapse here (`sed 'sx\x5bxYx'` is the pattern `x5b` in GNU and, since the
    escape pass, `Unmatched [` here -- it used to be a wrong line, which is why
    this landing makes the shape louder rather than newer), and `&` as an `s///`
    delimiter is the reverse: GNU KEEPS the backslash there, so `sed 's&x&\&&'`
    emits a literal `&` where td-txt emits the whole match. A backslash as an
    ADDRESS delimiter (`sed -n '\\^\p'`) is a third: GNU ends the address at the
    backslash, td-txt reports an unterminated regex. Four xfail cases. Fixing
    means porting `match_slash`'s condition rather than approximating it.

  - A backreference to a group under a repetition whose BODY can match empty,
    which POSIX leaves undefined and where GNU's answers do not follow a rule.
    Participation itself is modelled -- the body runs once so `^(x*)*\1$` and
    `^(x*)?\1$` select an empty line while `^(x)*\1$` does not -- but GNU splits
    the family in a way nothing here predicts: `*`, `?`, `{0,1}` and `{1}`
    participate, and `+`, `{0,2}`, `{1,2}` and `{2}` do not, so GNU selects an
    empty line for `^(x*)*\1$` and refuses it for `^(x*)+\1$`. Over the 81
    body/quantifier/subject shapes probed, main answered 22 wrongly and this
    landing answers 15, regressing none of them; the remaining 15 are that split.
    Encoding it would be a lookup table keyed on the bounds with no principle
    behind it, so what is implemented is the engine rule instead: an empty
    iteration runs once, to record participation, and never twice.

  - A `$` before a BARE `|` in a BRE, where the pipe is an ordinary character
    (`a|b` selects the text `a|b` in both tools). GNU stops reading the `$` as the
    literal it is everywhere else: `grep 'x$|*'` selects a line `x` and NOT a line
    `x$`, and sed's `s@x$|*@<&>@` matches NEITHER -- so the anchor reading and the
    literal reading are each contradicted by one of the two, and no third reading
    covers both. Fifteen probes, no model. At the START of a pattern the literal
    reading holds and both tools agree (`s@$|*@<&>@` replaces the `$` in `a$`).
    Six xfail cases carrying the goldens. This one is PRE-EXISTING -- the binary
    built from the parent commit answers identically -- and it is a different
    axis (when an anchor is an anchor); it is recorded here because a reviewer's
    cross-product found it while reviewing this branch, not because this branch
    caused it.

  - A token grep DROPS for having nothing to repeat, sitting immediately before a
    `)`. `(a\b*)`, `(\b*)`, `(z|\b*)` and `({)` are `Unmatched ( or \(` there and
    td-txt compiles them, while `(\b*a)` and `(a{*)` -- the same tokens with
    something after them -- are fine in both. So it is the `)`, not the group and
    not the quantifier; the earlier draft of this entry said "inside a group" and
    was wrong. Only `*`/`+`/`?` do it, never `{n}`. Pre-existing (main answers
    identically), and it is the same rule this landing DID implement for a branch
    emptied by dropping -- implementing it uniformly would close these xfails and
    `({)` together.

  - WHAT FOLLOWS a sed BRE assertion-plus-operator, where the parse rule above is
    uniform and glibc's MATCHER is not. `s|x\b\?|<&>|` selects the `x?` in `x?y`
    and `s|x\b\?y|<&>|` -- the same pattern with a `y` appended -- selects nothing
    at all, from any input; so do `x\b\?[y]`, `x\b\?[qy]`, `x\b\?\w`, `x\b\?yz`
    and `x\b\?y$`, while `x\b\?.`, `x\b\?y*` and `x\b\?[^q]` match. `x\b*$` and
    `x\b*\'` fail too, so it is not a matter of the follower being mandatory or
    single-byte -- an earlier draft of this entry said both and a reviewer showed
    counterexamples in each direction. The follower decides and no property that
    survives probing has been found: the nearest is "the follower can also match
    the operator character" (`x\b*[*qy]` matches, `x\b*[qy]` does not), and
    `x\b*.y` on `x*zy` breaks that too. Two xfail cases; 78 of the 2400 patterns
    in the sed-BRE grid, the other three dialects being 0 of 2400 each.

  - A BACKREFERENCE anywhere in a grep pattern, which switches the whole of it to
    glibc's reading -- that is, to sed's. `grep '\(.\)\1*\b*'` selects exactly
    what `sed 's|\(.\)\1*\b*|[&]|'` marks, while the same pattern WITHOUT the
    `\1` selects every line, the repeat reading modelled above. The minimal
    witness is `grep '\(a\)\1\b*'` on `aa`, which GNU does not select and
    td-txt does; it holds in the ERE too. This is grep choosing between its own
    dfa.c and glibc per PATTERN, and the same split shows up per INVOCATION:
    `grep -o` uses glibc where `-c` and `-q` use the dfa, so GNU itself answers
    `-c '\b*a'` and `-o '\b*a'` differently on `xab` -- the corpus pins `-c`,
    and td-txt, having one engine, cannot satisfy both. Three cases carry the
    goldens. The landing's own METHOD warning applies to its own model here: a
    corner probed through one frame confirms whatever that frame implies.

    This gap is where an earlier draft of this landing went wrong, and the shape
    of the mistake is worth keeping. Every probe of the corner used a follower of
    exactly that form (`x\B\?b`, `x\B\+*y`), so the quirk looked like the RULE:
    the model became "the assertion and its operator collapse into an atom that
    never matches, and a later zero-minimum operator brings the assertion back",
    which fitted forty probes and was still wrong. Dropping the follower is what
    showed it -- `x\b*` matches where `x\b*y` does not. A corner probed only
    through one syntactic frame will confirm whatever that frame implies.

  Goldens are C-locale. Under LC_ALL=C — the only locale td's image sets, and what
  every case here was captured under — GNU `grep -w '\.*'` selects `.a`; under a
  UTF-8 locale the same GNU binary selects nothing. Re-derive a `-w` golden without
  LC_ALL=C and it will look as though the corpus pins non-GNU behavior. It does
  not; it pins GNU in the locale td runs.

  Format: see the module header of ../src/lib.rs. `#### desc` opens a case,
  `## argv:` gives the command (argv[0] is the applet), `## stdin:`…`## END`
  and friends carry verbatim blocks, and `-json` spellings carry exact bytes
  where a block cannot (no trailing newline, a NUL).

  Provenance of the goldens: every case in grep-cli.test.txt and
  sed-cli.test.txt was RUN against the GNU program under LC_ALL=C, and its real
  stdout/status recorded here — GNU grep 3.11 and GNU sed 4.9, both from the
  authoring host's store. So these are upstream goldens too, just captured by
  running rather than copied from a file. To re-derive one by hand:

      printf '<the stdin block>' | grep <the argv, minus argv[0]>; echo $?

  divergence.test.txt is the exception, and says so in its own header: those
  goldens are td-txt's, pinning behavior it chooses differently on purpose (no
  shell-executing `sed e`, no PCRE `grep -P`, no `sed --debug`). Its cases are
  the record of those decisions.


Overlay
-------
  expectations.txt   td-txt's known-gap manifest (xfail / skip). Not a case file.
                     GENERATED, not hand-edited, by ../examples/gen_expectations.rs.
                     See its header for the xfail/skip semantics. Regenerate on a
                     pin bump or when passes change:

                       cargo build --release --manifest-path td-txt/Cargo.toml
                       cargo run --release --manifest-path td-txt/Cargo.toml \
                         --example gen_expectations -- \
                         td-txt/target/release/td-txt td-txt/spec > td-txt/spec/expectations.txt

                     It is EMPTY today: td-txt passes every case in this corpus.
                     That is a statement about the corpus as much as about
                     td-txt — growing the corpus is how the next gap gets found.
                     The usage-derived block above is one such growth, and it
                     found four (see the landing commit): `sed -i` with no file
                     operand, `-i` following symlinks and sharing hard links,
                     `--version`/`--help`, and Rust's `(os error N)` suffix on
                     every io diagnostic.

                     The `rootcheck td-volume` block found NONE: td-txt already
                     answered all 17 exactly as GNU grep 3.11 did. Those cases
                     earn their place as the score for a predicate that MOVED
                     onto this binary, not as a bug report — an ERE standing in
                     for an awk field test is worth pinning either way.

                     The fuzz-derived blocks found THREE, all wrong answers
                     rather than refusals, all now fixed: `-w` tested only the
                     greedy span at each start; BRE `^^` treated the second `^`
                     as a second anchor; and the matcher explored every end only
                     for patterns containing an alternation, so a bounded repeat
                     that needed an earlier greedy one to give ground came back
                     short. Hand-written cases had not reached any of the three
                     in 783 tries; a random search reached all three in a few
                     thousand. That is the argument for repeating the campaign
                     rather than only adding cases by reading the option list.

                     A FOURTH came from review rather than from either: `-w`
                     over an empty pattern, on the `match_all` path the fuzzer's
                     grammar could not reach. Same rule, second implementation
                     of it — which is the more useful lesson than the bug.

                     A FIFTH and SIXTH came from reviewing the FIX for the third,
                     which is the more instructive pair. Exploring every end made
                     ordinary long-line patterns report `too complex`; and the
                     empty-pattern short circuit, while being rewritten, was seen
                     to discard the rest of the pattern list, so `grep -w -e ''
                     -e ab` missed what GNU selects. The long-line block and the
                     mixed-pattern block are their scores. Both were invisible to
                     a corpus of short lines and single patterns, and the first
                     was a REGRESSION the oracle alone could not name — GNU and
                     the previous td-txt agreed, and only the new one differed.

                     A fourth divergence was pinned instead of fixed, in
                     divergence.test.txt: an anchor inside a `\+`-repeated group,
                     where GNU reports the empty span and td-txt the longer one.
                     POSIX does not define an anchor there, so matching GNU would
                     mean special-casing its artifact.
